//! util.sort — the one sort: stable.
//!
//! Ports sagitta `src/util/sort.c` (68 lines) and `sort.h` at 9ace98c2:
//! `yew_sort_stable`, a bottom-up mergesort with a scratch buffer, the
//! only sort in the tree. Why (`s00` §7): `qsort` is not stable and
//! glibc and musl disagree on tie order, so raw `qsort` is banned repo-
//! wide — a cross-libc nondeterminism in completion ranking, finder
//! results and tab ordering.
//!
//! wolf-std's `std.list.sort_by` is the same algorithm (a bottom-up
//! stable merge, "a tie takes from the left run", `std/list/list.lu`
//! and `std/sort/sort.lu`'s header) over a `less(a, b)` relation; this
//! unit is the port's one name for it, so a reader meets sagitta's rule
//! at one site. The C comparator's `ctx` has no twin: a wolf closure
//! captures what it needs.

use std.list

/// Sorts `xs` in place, stably: elements that compare equal keep their
/// relative order (`sort.c:38-68`). `less(a, b)` reads "a may precede
/// b" and must be a strict weak order.
pub fn sort_stable[T](mut xs: List[T], less: fn(T, T) -> bool) {
    list.sort_by(mut xs, less)
}
